#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105615 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59392c4-996f-5b29-9c37-7a1b75ffd896" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Positively Oriented Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "4b8266ba-b293-541f-9e4f-5005a6184816" :question/math-academy-id "e-9566" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $x^2+y^2 = 2$. Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C$." :question/worked-solution "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$.\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}x^{′}(t) \\\\ y^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2}\\sin t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\cos t\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\mid \\mid \\mathbf r'(t) \\mid \\mid = \\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{2}\\sin t)^2 + (\\sqrt{2}\\cos t)^2} = \\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nand since for our curve\n\n$$\nx(t) = \\sqrt{2}\\cos{t}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny(t) = \\sqrt{2}\\sin{t}\n$$\n\nit follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= (1)/(\\mid \\mid r^{′}(t) \\mid \\mid)\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2}\\cos t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\sin t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2}\\cos t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\sin t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105616 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad522514-328e-5944-b4c7-91d7e3b302f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Negatively Oriented Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "ad8da3b7-7e57-58bb-a0d2-8cdbddf80239" :question/math-academy-id "e-9571" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $\\mathbf r(t) = 12\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 9\\cos t \\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $[MATH: t \\in [0,2\\pi).]$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C$.\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $\\mathbf r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$.\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}x^{′}(t) \\\\ y^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}12\\cos t \\\\ -9\\sin t\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= (1)/(\\mid \\mid r^{′}(t) \\mid \\mid)\\begin{bmatrix}9\\sin t \\\\ 12\\cos t\\end{bmatrix} = \\frac{9\\sin ti + 12\\cos tj}{\\sqrt{144\\cos^{2} t + 81\\sin^{2} t}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105966 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad522514-328e-5944-b4c7-91d7e3b302f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Negatively Oriented Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b10faf1c-90b3-5a7c-a578-3d04dcc5bdaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\cos t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102044} {:answer/id #uuid "330860e8-f7c6-516a-9777-3e8301627fab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\sin t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102045} {:answer/id #uuid "511b7773-28b6-5728-9cc7-095802b34ba2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\sin t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102046} {:answer/id #uuid "58ce2e71-d841-5ebb-882a-fc2fbdc26b2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7\\cos t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102047} {:answer/id #uuid "75ad3d7b-8d9e-567e-91b3-ff03d3eeda41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24\\cos t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b10faf1c-90b3-5a7c-a578-3d04dcc5bdaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\cos t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102044} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd1c69f7-0887-5669-9c72-f0a7a3dc56cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102043}] :question/id #uuid "62471caf-151d-5553-a12b-9dfaa035554e" :question/math-academy-id "q-126826" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t) = 7\\sin ti + 24\\cos tj$ for $[MATH: t ∈ [0, 2π).]$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$n = (a(t)i + b(t)j)/(\\mid \\mid r^{′}(t) \\mid \\mid)$.\nFind the function $b(t)$.\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105967 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59392c4-996f-5b29-9c37-7a1b75ffd896" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Positively Oriented Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99f7b9ab-2895-5c57-9c75-8d6b8297740f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3x \\\\ 3y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102050} {:answer/id #uuid "a9636677-7c14-57f1-910d-66fb8733aea6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102051} {:answer/id #uuid "dc408540-0c01-5568-aa45-f522fe602c71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}y \\\\ -x\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102052} {:answer/id #uuid "df16b44e-806d-5830-b5ec-407e2468a418" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102053} {:answer/id #uuid "77677902-7c56-5ae8-b1d9-1262045afd8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77677902-7c56-5ae8-b1d9-1262045afd8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102054} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1eca71e-3deb-504b-9569-e86615218d4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102049}] :question/id #uuid "6ad4fbe5-37f0-5999-9ae5-70bbcca8a444" :question/math-academy-id "q-126830" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $x^{2} + y^{2} = 9$. Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C$."}]}
